    "title": "Panamanian Folkloric Group keeps culture alive in Hampton Roads - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Jimmy LaRoue ,\n4 hours ago\nHAMPTON, Va. (WAVY) — The sound of rhythmic footwork and vibrant chants fill the attic of Phoebus United Methodist Church, where the Panamanian Folkloric Group of Hampton Roads gathers to rehearse.\nThe group in Newport News was founded in 1992 by Aura Robinson, who moved to Virginia after her husband was relocated due to the military. Robinson first formed a folkloric dance troupe in Oklahoma in 1979 and brought that passion with her to Hampton Roads.\n“My husband is in the military, and we decided to retire here,” Robinson said.\nSince then, the group has been a space where Panamanian culture is celebrated and shared with the wider community. Robinson even reunited with a former member from her Oklahoma troupe after moving to Newport News.\n“We started performing in 1992 and we haven’t stopped,” Robinson said. “We haven’t stopped dancing.”\nFor members like Janio Anderson, the group is about unity and pride.\n“I can see that all of us have one thing in common: same culture, same food, same spirit, same everything,” Anderson said. “That’s why we are unified.”\nThe dancers wear polleras, Panama’s traditional dress, often paired with handcrafted tembleques headdresses.\nRobinson says that tradition is deeply personal.\n“Our pollera is the most beautiful thing for me,” she said in Spanish. “… I’m an older lady now, but when I put my pollera on, I feel like a little girl again.”\nWhile the Hispanic community in Newport News and Hampton is growing every year, Robinson says the group has created a home for tradition and family. Many members also attend church in the same building where they rehearse.\nCopyright 2025 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to WAVY.com.",
